WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.830
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.740
So in this lecture, you want
to look at hadron colliders

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:16.620
very briefly as an introductory
lecture to this topic,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.210
but also as the conclusion of
the discussion we had in QCD.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.600
So historically speaking,
hadron colliders

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.310
might have been
the tool in order

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.500
to probe the energy frontier.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.650
So, and that has to do with
accelerator technology,

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:35.270
the fact that heavy particles
emit less synchrotron radiation

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:36.890
allows them in
circular colliders

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.480
to be collided at
higher energies.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:44.060
This plot here shows
as a function of year

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.800
the energy of the constituents.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.710
So these are the
elementary particles

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.420
used in the interaction, or the
energy of the quarks and gluons

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.910
being part of the interaction.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.390
And you see that
typically we find

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.970
that hadron colliders,
here in red,

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.209
have an edge over lepton
colliders in terms

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:07.350
of the maximum collision energy.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.380
So the energy
frontier usually is

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.630
given by the hadron
collider as compared

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.300
to the lepton colliders.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.940
The protocol is a
Livingston plot,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.350
and it's a little bit old.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.130
We are now here in 2020.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.780
We haven't built
this machine yet.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:29.350
We might build a lepton collider
at 250 GB in 10 years from now

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:31.460
or 15 years from now.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:38.950
And also, the LHC is very
stable at this energy here.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.260
But the point of
this lecture is more

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:44.560
to discuss how we can make
a cross-section calculation

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.390
of important photon collisions.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.040
So we have already
seen how we can

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:52.760
make this cross-section
calculation where we have,

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:58.670
let's say, an initial quark
and an anti-quark colliding

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.535
with exchange of a photon.

00:02:00.535 --> 00:02:02.990
We haven't seen how we can
do this with a Z boson.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.090
But we'll see that next week.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.115
This process is called
Drell-Yan production.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.230
And we're looking at the decay
either of the photon--virtual,

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:21.580
so virtual photon, or the Z
boson and a pair of leptons,

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.950
electrons, and muons at highest.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.450
So we can calculate this.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:29.140
And we call this cross-section
the hard scattering

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.880
cross-section--
the cross-section

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.960
of this hard scattering process.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:36.300
But we need to, in
order to calculate this,

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.880
know the momentum
distribution and the abundance

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:42.790
of the initial quarks
and anti-quarks.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.590
And so we do this
using the structure

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.715
from the parton
distribution functions,

00:02:47.715 --> 00:02:50.410
as we discussed them before.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:51.580
We have to integrate.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:55.000
And those are labeled
here with those q's.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.420
We have to integrate them
over all possible momentum.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:04.960
And we have to
sum over the quark

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.150
species inside the proton.

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:09.400
For this process, we don't
have to consider the gluon.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.910
Because of leading order, we
cannot produce a lepton pair

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:13.900
with gluons.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.740
Higher orders, we also have
to consider gluon densities

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.960
in this discussion.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:26.520
And then the momentum
of the parton collision

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.010
has to be equal
to the momentum--

00:03:31.638 --> 00:03:34.510
the center of mass
energy considered

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.500
in the parton cross-section.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.010
This process, this technique,
here is called factorization.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:43.040
So we factorize the
hard shattering process

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.250
from the structure
of the proton in

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.240
the cross-section calculation.

